    "lawCode": "Amd §100, add Art 17 §§350 - 354, Ec Dev L",
    "lawSection": "Economic Development Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5419\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the economic development law, in relation to\nestablishing the academic research information access act\n\n\n PURPOSE OF THE BILL :\nThis bill establishes the Academic Research Information Access Act\n(ARIA) to foster innovations and economic growth by broadening the\navailability of high end information resources.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n§ 1- Sets out the short title.\n\n§ 2- Legislative findings. This bill creates an appropriation to allow\npublic and private academic research libraries to make their\ncollections accessible to the entrepreneurial community. Currently\naccess is limited because of the high cost of licensing agreements and\nrestrictive contracts that inhibit collaboration.\n\n§ 3- Amends section 100 of the Economic Development Law which sets out\nthe general powers of the Department to include promoting the broad\navailability of information resources.\n\n§ 4- Adds a new article 17 to the Economic Development Law entitled\nAcademic Research Information Access.\n\n§350 - General. Establishes the academic research information access\n(ARIA) program in the Department of Economic Development.\n\n§351 - Information Resources. Requires the Department in conjunction\nwith SUNY and the NYS Board of Regents to establish contracts and\nlicenses for the provision of electronic resources (including\nacademic, professional and industry journals, reference handbooks and\nmanuals, research tracking tools, indexes and abstracts). The users of\nthese resources shall be: individuals operating primarily in the\nfields of science, technology and medical research and development,\nincluding researchers, scholars, faculty and students and member and\nassociate institutions of the New York State Higher Education\nInitiative (NYSHEI); researchers; entrepreneurs; innovators existing\nin business incubators aligned with a NYSHEI member; recipients of\ngrants from NYSTAR or Empire State Development; and small businesses\nin approved empire zones.\n\n§ 352 -  Resource selection. Resources made available through this\nprogram will be selected from recommendations made by an advisory\nboard of public and private library representatives.\n\n§ 353 - Contractual Negotiations. Agreements funded by ARIA shall be\nnegotiated in compliance with state procurement contracting\nprocedures.\n\n§ 354 - Administration - Gives the Department the Authority to\npromulgate rules and regulations to implement this program.\n\n§ 6- Effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nTo foster a knowledge-based, or innovation, economy in New York it is\nessential to create an information infrastructure to support academic,\nbusiness and entrepreneurial research, discovery and growth.  The\nearly twenty-first century has rightly been termed the \"information\nage.\" Scholarly information driven by new research is generated at the\nfastest pace in history; and it has become a major, if not the\nprincipal component of a knowledge-based economy. A 1993 U.S. Commerce\nDepartment report notes that two-thirds of American workers are in\ninformation related jobs, and the rest are in industries that are\nreliant on information. The report states plainly that information\nfuels economic growth and creates jobs.  In an information\ninfrastructure, access to academic research and development\ninformation resources is critical. Researchers confirm that access to\nhigh-end information resources leads to 87 percent more success in\nresearch activities. This leads to more patents, more licenses, and\nthus more jobs and economic strength. In recent years, New York has\nmade considerable investments in promoting research and innovation as\na stimulus to the economy. Missing from past efforts has been broad\nstatewide access to electronic resources essential to research and\neconomic development. It is noteworthy that the most substantive\nprogress in establishing information infrastructure to support\nteaching, learning, research and innovation occurs in places other\nthan New York. Nearly half of all other states have created some\nvariety of a library-driven information infrastructure.  Academic\nlibraries in other states have been successful in obtaining state\ngovernment support of high-end research databases in scientific,\ntechnical engineering, medical and financial fields of endeavor.\nThrough State contracts, individual academic libraries have benefited\nfrom deep savings in the costs of these databases, enabling them to\ninvest those savings to extend their local database offerings to the\ngrowing and emerging fields of study so important to their regional\neconomies.  Additionally, only through state contracts can these\ninformation resources by shared with small business interests, such as\nthose being cultivated in Empire Zones or various incubators.  Through\na state supported program for research and development information\nresources, college and university faculty are able to contribute\nadvanced research to industry and government.  This widespread access\nwill fuel workforce development, economic expansion, new enterprises,\nnew inventions, new industries and new jobs.\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nNone.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2007-2008: A.9672/S.6741.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nYet to be determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
